1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:06,840
For at least 3,000 years, emperors and generals, dictators and police, criminals, clerics,

2
00:00:07,080 --> 00:00:11,160
and even medical doctors have used a vast array of torture devices.

3
00:00:14,040 --> 00:00:20,500
These insidious instruments included the mundane and the exotic, everything from fists and feet

4
00:00:20,501 --> 00:00:24,820
to elaborate machines that literally slow-cooked their victims alive.

5
00:00:24,821 --> 00:00:33,320
Wooden crosses were favored by the ancient Romans, various spiked metal encasements,

6
00:00:33,321 --> 00:00:38,820
stretching machines, and sexual devices by the medieval Europeans, fists, beatings, and

7
00:00:38,821 --> 00:00:47,240
electricity by 21st century torturers.

8
00:00:47,241 --> 00:00:52,340
When it comes to a definition of torture and torture devices, there is little disagreement

9
00:00:52,341 --> 00:00:53,340
among experts.

10
00:00:53,341 --> 00:01:00,540
Under the UN Convention Against Torture, which was passed by the General Assembly in 1984,

11
00:01:01,320 --> 00:01:09,500
torture is defined as cruel, inhuman, or degrading activities against an individual

12
00:01:09,501 --> 00:01:16,320
for the purpose of extracting information or punishment or revenge or intimidation.

13
00:01:16,321 --> 00:01:25,320
A torture instrument is essentially any instrument that's used to inflict forcible pain on a human being.

14
00:01:27,100 --> 00:01:29,500
What kind of person tortures another human being?

15
00:01:30,080 --> 00:01:35,360
And who among us would be inspired to create an instrument of torture and then actually use it?

16
00:01:36,480 --> 00:01:40,140
People often imagine that the torturer is some sort of psychopath.

17
00:01:40,141 --> 00:01:47,140
But in fact, torturers can be highly educated people who come out of universities, who have studied music and art and philosophy,

18
00:01:47,141 --> 00:01:55,140
and they can also be brutal people who come out of the prisons and are used by the state to act as torturers.

19
00:01:57,140 --> 00:02:04,140
Over the past decade, the United Nations has openly denounced the use of any torture for any reason by any nation state.

20
00:02:04,141 --> 00:02:07,180
any nation state.

21
00:02:07,181 --> 00:02:13,380
But what about cases like the capture of the Al Qaeda military commander Khalid Sheikh Mohammed,

22
00:02:13,381 --> 00:02:20,140
a man who was one of Bin Laden's closest associates and Al Qaeda's number one strategist?

23
00:02:20,141 --> 00:02:24,820
Would torture be justified in an attempt to extract vital information from them about

24
00:02:24,821 --> 00:02:30,840
future acts of terrorism?

25
00:02:30,841 --> 00:02:34,600
to gain the names and locations of other terrorists in his vast network.

26
00:02:43,620 --> 00:02:48,480
For anyone that thinks that torture is actually an effective device to get quality information,

27
00:02:49,260 --> 00:02:55,580
that's certainly never been proven by any experience in humankind for centuries.

28
00:02:56,520 --> 00:03:01,000
Torture gets people to say anything to make the pain go away.

29
00:03:02,940 --> 00:03:07,360
Experts tell us that the same is true when it comes to extracting confessions to a crime.

30
00:03:08,620 --> 00:03:12,600
Confessions extracted under torture are notoriously bad.

31
00:03:14,440 --> 00:03:18,420
I can get anyone to confess to anything.

32
00:03:19,060 --> 00:03:24,460
If you give me enough time, I bet you I can get you to confess to anything I want you to confess to.

33
00:03:24,461 --> 00:03:27,760
It's just a matter of time because people have a breaking point.

34
00:03:30,900 --> 00:03:34,420
In fact, in early 2003, in the state of Illinois,

35
00:03:35,160 --> 00:03:38,340
Governor George Ryan commuted the sentences of prison inmates

36
00:03:38,341 --> 00:03:41,280
whose convictions were largely based on confessions

37
00:03:41,281 --> 00:03:43,300
extracted as a result of torture,

38
00:03:44,080 --> 00:03:46,840
torture that included electronic shocking devices.

39
00:03:51,360 --> 00:03:54,020
These electronic immobilization devices,

40
00:03:54,021 --> 00:03:57,501
or EIDs, were originally developed in the 1970s.

41
00:03:57,502 --> 00:04:00,620
They were designed to be used as non-lethal

42
00:04:00,621 --> 00:04:05,300
restraint devices for police forces and prison guards who were dealing with dangerous criminals.

43
00:04:06,120 --> 00:04:12,200
They are still lawful for this purpose, but over the last 30 years, they have risen to prominence

44
00:04:12,201 --> 00:04:16,960
as illegal torture devices for a number of governments, military organizations,

45
00:04:17,360 --> 00:04:21,060
and police agencies who are trying to hide their torture activities.

46
00:04:21,061 --> 00:04:29,300
What a lot of the electric shack technology does now is it doesn't leave permanent marks.

47
00:04:30,140 --> 00:04:37,740
And so it enables torturers to engage in or to inflict the same kind of intense pain

48
00:04:37,741 --> 00:04:42,940
to accomplish their objectives so they can deny that they engaged in torture.

49
00:04:45,580 --> 00:04:49,120
Among the convenient electronic tools designed for one purpose

50
00:04:49,121 --> 00:04:51,360
but turned into a device for mistreating humans

51
00:04:51,361 --> 00:04:52,760
was the cattle prod,

52
00:04:53,340 --> 00:04:55,720
which first appeared in the stockyards of Buenos Aires

53
00:04:55,721 --> 00:04:57,440
in the early 1930s.

54
00:04:59,700 --> 00:05:04,160
It was nothing more than a billy club device

55
00:05:04,161 --> 00:05:06,580
that was initially used, quite literally,

56
00:05:06,700 --> 00:05:09,380
to prod cattle to get them to move along.

57
00:05:09,381 --> 00:05:16,640
It was modified then into a device to prod people,

58
00:05:16,641 --> 00:05:21,600
a riot control device during the riots of the 1960s

59
00:05:21,601 --> 00:05:25,380
where you saw it used against people

60
00:05:25,381 --> 00:05:28,440
who were marching for their rights,

61
00:05:29,220 --> 00:05:32,680
used by police forces in the South

62
00:05:32,681 --> 00:05:34,640
against poor black folks

63
00:05:34,641 --> 00:05:37,760
that were marching to be American citizens.

64
00:05:42,640 --> 00:05:45,560
A modern cattle prod delivers a mild shock

65
00:05:45,561 --> 00:05:50,600
at the point of physical contact by means of a low-voltage electric current sent across two

66
00:05:50,601 --> 00:05:51,530
electrodes.

67
00:05:51,531 --> 00:05:58,040
Experts say the small amount of electricity does not harm cattle, but it does

68
00:05:58,041 --> 00:06:02,838
deliver a reasonably painful shock to the smaller human body.

69
00:06:02,839 --> 00:06:05,040
Stun guns are another story.

70
00:06:06,440 --> 00:06:14,520
A stun gun is a small, very concealable, handheld device that enables an individual

71
00:06:14,521 --> 00:06:21,520
individual who is in close physical proximity to another to deliver a debilitating charge

72
00:06:21,521 --> 00:06:23,106
of electricity to them.

73
00:06:23,107 --> 00:06:27,520
You actually have to physically make contact with the individual

74
00:06:27,521 --> 00:06:33,520
and this device to generally incapacitate them.

75
00:06:33,521 --> 00:06:38,663
About the size of a typical flashlight, stun guns have a fairly simple design.

76
00:06:38,664 --> 00:06:39,520
They generate

77
00:06:39,521 --> 00:06:43,760
generate a high voltage, low amperage electrical charge.

78
00:06:43,761 --> 00:06:46,600
The power source is ordinary 9-volt batteries

79
00:06:46,601 --> 00:06:48,500
that supply electricity to a circuit

80
00:06:48,501 --> 00:06:50,740
that includes multiple transformers.

81
00:06:50,741 --> 00:06:53,240
These components boost the voltage in the circuit

82
00:06:53,241 --> 00:06:57,900
anywhere from 80,000 volts to a high of 720,000 volts,

83
00:06:57,901 --> 00:07:01,080
enough electricity to trigger 1,000 garage door openers.

84
00:07:09,520 --> 00:07:20,900
Stun belts are basically stun guns that are attached to prisoners by means of a heavy-duty, one-size-fits-all belt.

85
00:07:22,280 --> 00:07:28,000
There is a remote control unit that activates this device from as far a distance as 200 to 300 feet.

86
00:07:29,780 --> 00:07:35,160
When it's engaged, a stun belt will administer a high-voltage charge to the inmate's kidneys.

87
00:07:35,161 --> 00:07:40,240
An instantaneous shock spreads throughout the entire body,

88
00:07:40,241 --> 00:07:43,160
and the prisoner becomes paralyzed for several minutes.

89
00:07:43,161 --> 00:07:49,840
The immediate effects of electric torture can include rupturing of blood vessels

90
00:07:49,841 --> 00:07:54,580
and tearing and rupturing of muscles and ligaments off the bone.

91
00:07:55,100 --> 00:07:59,680
The long-term effects really depend on the system of the body that's affected.

92
00:07:59,681 --> 00:08:07,860
It's the kind of pain that causes people to urinate or defecate without control, to lose control of their muscles.

93
00:08:08,900 --> 00:08:14,920
It's the kind of pain that causes people to have ongoing mental health problems.

94
00:08:17,560 --> 00:08:23,500
In some countries, these more sophisticated electronic immobilization devices are difficult to obtain.

95
00:08:24,820 --> 00:08:28,720
As a result, torturers will use a cheap makeshift version.

96
00:08:28,721 --> 00:08:32,920
a couple of high-voltage batteries connected to a few cables or wires.

97
00:08:34,060 --> 00:08:39,500
One of the ways that torture has often been perpetrated,

98
00:08:39,640 --> 00:08:42,180
particularly against political dissidents,

99
00:08:42,660 --> 00:08:48,560
has been the use of electricity where electrical wires have been attached to genitals

100
00:08:48,561 --> 00:08:54,980
or fingers or toes or a person's head using a generator or battery

101
00:08:54,981 --> 00:08:57,560
or any sort of electrical device.

102
00:08:57,561 --> 00:09:02,200
often what happens is that a person is splashed with water beforehand

103
00:09:02,201 --> 00:09:06,820
so that the electrical current will run easier into the body.

104
00:09:09,820 --> 00:09:13,960
The use of these devices to terrorize, to punish,

105
00:09:14,380 --> 00:09:18,360
to extract information and confessions, leaves no visible trace.

106
00:09:19,100 --> 00:09:22,520
But the torture instruments of antiquity were not so deceptive.

107
00:09:27,560 --> 00:09:35,740
In the 21st century, governments claim that the practice of torture is despicable and horrifying.

108
00:09:37,000 --> 00:09:40,620
But for at least three millennia, torture was legal.

109
00:09:41,580 --> 00:09:47,320
It was a commonly used method of punishment, not only for criminals, but also for innocent slaves and foreigners.

110
00:09:47,321 --> 00:09:52,460
over 3,000 years ago

111
00:09:52,461 --> 00:09:54,360
the Chinese book of wisdom

112
00:09:54,361 --> 00:09:55,500
known as the I Ching

113
00:09:55,501 --> 00:09:58,180
spoke of a punishment called the Shi Ho

114
00:09:58,181 --> 00:09:59,020
in this way

115
00:09:59,021 --> 00:10:02,180
Shin Ho, biting through

116
00:10:02,181 --> 00:10:04,640
his neck is locked in the wooden Kong

117
00:10:04,641 --> 00:10:06,260
his ears are gone

118
00:10:06,261 --> 00:10:07,600
great misfortune

119
00:10:07,601 --> 00:10:12,520
this punishment required a device called the Kong

120
00:10:12,521 --> 00:10:15,580
it was used for minor criminal offenses

121
00:10:15,581 --> 00:10:17,020
well into the 20th century

122
00:10:17,021 --> 00:10:24,020
The Kong was a very large, heavy, thick wooden board with a hole cut in the middle the size of the victim's neck.

123
00:10:24,021 --> 00:10:30,020
This bulky device was worn for as long as 20 or 30 days.

124
00:10:30,021 --> 00:10:33,020
It was never allowed off the victim's neck.

125
00:10:33,021 --> 00:10:38,020
Because of the board's size, it was impossible for the victim to feed himself,

126
00:10:38,021 --> 00:10:42,020
making him dependent upon the kindness of others for food and drink.

127
00:10:42,021 --> 00:10:45,020
If he had no friends, he could die from starvation.

128
00:10:47,020 --> 00:10:56,960
In 1300 BC, in the Mediterranean world, the Egyptian pharaoh Ramses II is said to have

129
00:10:56,961 --> 00:11:02,020
tortured Hittite prisoners by flogging them with whips made of ox leather, weighted with

130
00:11:02,021 --> 00:11:06,900
bone splitters or metal hooks and spikes.

131
00:11:06,901 --> 00:11:11,720
His purpose was to extract information about their battle plan against Egypt, and the location

132
00:11:11,721 --> 00:11:12,580
of their forces.

133
00:11:17,020 --> 00:11:20,060
The ancient Assyrians also practiced horrifying brutality,

134
00:11:20,061 --> 00:11:23,860
not just in warfare, but against their own dissident citizens.

135
00:11:25,020 --> 00:11:28,960
We have, for example, an ancient story of a village

136
00:11:28,961 --> 00:11:31,900
that tried to revolt against the Assyrian Empire.

137
00:11:32,420 --> 00:11:34,940
And the Assyrians rounded up the leaders of the revolt,

138
00:11:35,500 --> 00:11:40,500
flayed them alive, spread their skins on the city walls as a warning.

139
00:11:41,260 --> 00:11:44,580
We know this story from Assyrian sources.

140
00:11:44,581 --> 00:11:47,840
In other words, they wanted the story to be known.

141
00:11:48,460 --> 00:11:53,080
They wanted people to hear the horrific results of any who would oppose them.

142
00:11:53,540 --> 00:11:56,700
That is torture and terrorism.

143
00:11:59,140 --> 00:12:04,440
Nearly a thousand years later, it was the Greeks who brought their own brand of deadly ingenuity

144
00:12:04,441 --> 00:12:09,260
to the creation of one particular torture device, the brazen bull.

145
00:12:09,261 --> 00:12:15,880
This was a large copper bronze statue of a bull that was hollow.

146
00:12:16,620 --> 00:12:23,880
And according to the legend, according to the story, you would place someone inside this device and then heat it.

147
00:12:24,580 --> 00:12:31,460
The heat was created by fires that were lit underneath the hollow bronze figure, making the brazen bull function like an oven.

148
00:12:33,000 --> 00:12:39,000
The torture victim would slowly roast, as well as suffocate from the loss of oxygen caused by the tremendous heat.

149
00:12:39,260 --> 00:12:46,260
Supposedly, the groans of the person suffering inside would sound like the sound of a bull,

150
00:12:46,261 --> 00:12:50,260
the sound of a cow. It's a horrific thought.

151
00:12:50,261 --> 00:12:57,260
A few centuries later, the Romans became known for using torture with a vengeance throughout

152
00:12:57,261 --> 00:12:58,903
their far-flung empire.

153
00:12:58,904 --> 00:13:03,260
One of their most used torture devices was the flogging whip,

154
00:13:03,261 --> 00:13:08,100
whip, also known as the flagellum.

155
00:13:08,101 --> 00:13:11,360
Its thongs were made from tanned ox leather, the ends of which

156
00:13:11,361 --> 00:13:14,440
were weighted with lead balls.

157
00:13:14,441 --> 00:13:16,580
As the torturer whipped his prisoner,

158
00:13:16,581 --> 00:13:18,720
these lead weights cut deeply and painfully

159
00:13:18,721 --> 00:13:20,820
into his victim's flesh.

160
00:13:20,821 --> 00:13:22,320
It's the lacerations themselves

161
00:13:22,321 --> 00:13:26,740
that probably hurt the most in whipping and caning.

162
00:13:26,741 --> 00:13:31,320
It's the tearing of the flesh against the bone below

163
00:13:31,321 --> 00:13:32,711
that hurt so much.

164
00:13:32,712 --> 00:13:38,200
It was during the reign of Tiberius that the most famous torture victim

165
00:13:38,201 --> 00:13:42,134
of all time, Jesus of Nazareth, was crucified.

166
00:13:42,135 --> 00:13:45,640
Crucifixion as a form of public execution

167
00:13:45,641 --> 00:13:52,800
predates the Romans. We know, at least as early as the Persian Empire, some kind of

168
00:13:52,801 --> 00:13:57,600
public display of death that involves hanging up victims.

169
00:13:57,601 --> 00:14:00,800
The idea seems rather straightforward.

170
00:14:00,801 --> 00:14:05,860
make a person's death as publicly horrific as possible.

171
00:14:08,300 --> 00:14:11,240
The purpose of this was to send a powerful message

172
00:14:11,241 --> 00:14:13,200
to would-be dissidents and troublemakers.

173
00:14:13,880 --> 00:14:16,060
Do not defy the state's authority.

174
00:14:18,660 --> 00:14:21,300
Hundreds of years before Jesus' torture and execution,

175
00:14:22,120 --> 00:14:24,800
victims of crucifixion were tied, not nailed,

176
00:14:24,920 --> 00:14:28,340
to a single wooden stake that was planted firmly in the ground.

177
00:14:29,180 --> 00:14:32,040
They were left to die from thirst or from starvation.

178
00:14:32,640 --> 00:14:36,660
And there was always the possibility they would be attacked by hungry wild animals,

179
00:14:37,140 --> 00:14:38,340
searching for an easy meal.

180
00:14:40,320 --> 00:14:45,640
In approximately 70 BC, the Romans introduced the four-pointed cross

181
00:14:45,641 --> 00:14:47,940
that would later become the symbol of Christianity.

182
00:14:48,740 --> 00:14:50,940
But long before it was used to crucify Jesus,

183
00:14:51,560 --> 00:14:54,220
the Roman army made extensive use of their new cross

184
00:14:54,221 --> 00:14:57,340
when they crushed the slave rebellion led by the gladiator Spartacus

185
00:14:57,341 --> 00:15:02,194
in 71 BC. 6,000 of his followers were crucified.

186
00:15:02,195 --> 00:15:06,340
The Roman commander had their bodies hung

187
00:15:06,341 --> 00:15:12,340
from beams that lined the road from Capua, where the revolt began, to Rome, a distance

188
00:15:12,341 --> 00:15:18,340
of 270 miles. Spartacus himself was killed in the battle.

189
00:15:18,341 --> 00:15:25,340
When we think about who it was that Rome executed in these horrific public forum, like the

190
00:15:25,341 --> 00:15:27,540
like the crucifixion.

191
00:15:27,541 --> 00:15:29,180
It's clear to us.

192
00:15:29,181 --> 00:15:37,620
The Romans executed publicly anyone who was seen to oppose Roman power.

193
00:15:37,621 --> 00:15:41,780
Jesus of Nazareth was considered just such a threat.

194
00:15:41,781 --> 00:15:47,920
He was arrested during Passover, quickly tried and then convicted in the local Roman court.

195
00:15:47,921 --> 00:15:52,260
It was then that he was subjected to a series of tortures.

196
00:15:52,261 --> 00:15:59,260
There's little reason to doubt the New Testament picture that Jesus began suffering long before

197
00:15:59,261 --> 00:16:06,260
the actual crucifixion, that Jesus was whipped, that Jesus was beaten, that he was already

198
00:16:06,261 --> 00:16:14,600
cut and bleeding from crowns of thorns or from the whipping, and was made exhausted to carry

199
00:16:14,601 --> 00:16:17,900
the device of his own death.

200
00:16:17,901 --> 00:16:22,700
The flogging whip used on Jesus was the lead-weighted leather flagellum.

201
00:16:22,701 --> 00:16:26,620
The crown of thorns that was placed on his head may have been constructed from the branches

202
00:16:26,621 --> 00:16:32,720
of a plant with long curved thorns called the Zisyphus spinocristi that grows in the

203
00:16:32,721 --> 00:16:35,800
Jordan Valley.

204
00:16:35,801 --> 00:16:39,820
His final torture took place on the wooden cross.

205
00:16:39,821 --> 00:16:45,060
Crucifixion was designed to make death extended.

206
00:16:45,061 --> 00:16:53,300
That is, it was to inflict the maximum amount of pain and suffering and to make it last.

207
00:16:53,301 --> 00:16:58,580
Once at Golgotha, the location chosen for his crucifixion, iron spikes were driven through

208
00:16:58,581 --> 00:17:01,100
Jesus' wrists and feet.

209
00:17:01,101 --> 00:17:06,720
There was nothing in place to support his shoulders, neck, head or the bulk of his body.

210
00:17:06,721 --> 00:17:11,280
As a result of this positioning, the uppermost portion of his trunk eventually would collapse

211
00:17:11,281 --> 00:17:14,360
over his diaphragm, making normal breathing impossible.

212
00:17:16,040 --> 00:17:19,840
The best guess seems to be that people die from asphyxiation,

213
00:17:20,500 --> 00:17:24,300
unable to exhale because of the paralysis of the pectoral

214
00:17:24,301 --> 00:17:25,500
and intercostal muscles.

215
00:17:26,420 --> 00:17:30,880
So their lungs become filled with carbon dioxide

216
00:17:30,881 --> 00:17:33,320
and unable to take in oxygen.

217
00:17:33,700 --> 00:17:36,400
And eventually, they suffocate.

218
00:17:37,640 --> 00:17:40,940
The Romans continued crucifying and torturing Christians

219
00:17:40,941 --> 00:17:43,640
for the next 300 years.

220
00:17:43,641 --> 00:17:47,240
Then, ironically, the Roman Empire under Constantine

221
00:17:47,241 --> 00:17:52,280
embraced Christianity as its own state religion.

222
00:17:52,281 --> 00:17:55,680
But as the years passed, Christianity itself

223
00:17:55,681 --> 00:17:59,200
adopted the use of torture, sending the imaginations

224
00:17:59,201 --> 00:18:04,500
of inventors of torture devices to new levels of darkness.

225
00:18:06,500 --> 00:18:10,040
The creation of tribunals to suppress opposing viewpoints

226
00:18:10,041 --> 00:18:17,000
has a long tradition clerics kings and even united states senators have employed state

227
00:18:17,001 --> 00:18:21,480
sponsored courts or inquisitions to root out the heretics and non-believers

228
00:18:24,600 --> 00:18:30,440
in the 15th century it was the spanish royals isabella and ferdinand who gave what became

229
00:18:30,441 --> 00:18:36,280
known as the spanish inquisition its place in history as a harsh and deadly exercise of

230
00:18:36,281 --> 00:18:38,740
of combined church and state power,

231
00:18:38,741 --> 00:18:40,880
dedicated to torturing those citizens

232
00:18:40,881 --> 00:18:42,340
who it named as heretics.

233
00:18:44,100 --> 00:18:47,640
The state, or the secular, has always been involved

234
00:18:47,641 --> 00:18:49,280
in torture to some capacity.

235
00:18:49,281 --> 00:18:51,040
When we think about the Inquisition,

236
00:18:51,041 --> 00:18:53,860
we often associate it with the church,

237
00:18:53,861 --> 00:18:57,680
but the Inquisition was really the church and state

238
00:18:57,681 --> 00:19:01,820
working hand in hand, often for the purposes of the state

239
00:19:01,821 --> 00:19:03,200
more than the church.

240
00:19:06,280 --> 00:19:16,140
Under the guise of establishing unity in her homeland, Queen Isabella asked for and received the Roman Catholic Pope's permission to purify her subjects.

241
00:19:19,180 --> 00:19:22,600
Spanish citizens of every rank and class had three choices.

242
00:19:23,780 --> 00:19:31,600
To accept Roman Catholicism as their religion, to leave Spain, or to risk torture at the hands of the Inquisition.

243
00:19:31,601 --> 00:19:37,764
It only took two adult men to identify somebody as a heretic.

244
00:19:37,765 --> 00:19:41,300
At that point, they could be tried.

245
00:19:41,960 --> 00:19:45,880
But before a trial could begin, a confession from the accused was required.

246
00:19:47,200 --> 00:19:48,960
That's where torture became necessary.

247
00:19:49,960 --> 00:19:57,000
A typical torture session during the time of the Inquisition would often involve a number of different tools that would be present.

248
00:19:57,001 --> 00:20:00,380
and there might be a number of other people present participating.

249
00:20:01,140 --> 00:20:04,620
And it's very also important to note that there would be other victims

250
00:20:04,621 --> 00:20:09,400
who were both in the process of being tortured or yet to be tortured.

251
00:20:11,760 --> 00:20:17,600
The face of torture during the Inquisition was the Iron Maiden, manufactured in Germany.

252
00:20:18,640 --> 00:20:22,840
It was a man-sized container fitted on the inside with sharp metal spikes,

253
00:20:22,841 --> 00:20:28,980
a torture device that was well known and deeply feared not only in Spain but in all of Europe.

254
00:20:30,780 --> 00:20:35,580
There was a great deal of craftsmanship, ingenuity and knowledge of the human body

255
00:20:35,581 --> 00:20:37,740
to produce and design the Iron Maiden.

256
00:20:38,120 --> 00:20:44,200
As you open up, you'll notice that these spikes inside are placed in very strategic points

257
00:20:44,201 --> 00:20:46,580
intended to miss the vital organs.

258
00:20:47,040 --> 00:20:50,580
This was done to ensure the victim would remain alive as long as possible.

259
00:20:50,581 --> 00:20:54,500
all the while experiencing tremendous pain and suffering.

260
00:20:56,980 --> 00:20:59,980
While the Iron Maiden may have been the face of the Inquisition,

261
00:21:00,700 --> 00:21:04,000
it was generally thought that the rack was its workhorse.

262
00:21:04,320 --> 00:21:07,940
The rack is one of the most common forms of torture that we know

263
00:21:07,941 --> 00:21:14,640
in terms that you can use many different methods in your process, in your torture creation.

264
00:21:14,980 --> 00:21:18,580
The first element is the crank at the end.

265
00:21:18,581 --> 00:21:21,320
Now here is where you would tie the person's feet.

266
00:21:21,800 --> 00:21:27,080
You would slowly add more tension to the point of elongating the body.

267
00:21:27,640 --> 00:21:32,840
They would be rolled across these spikes that would be much sharper, of course, at the time.

268
00:21:33,620 --> 00:21:40,000
And then at this end, the hands would be locked down and really leaving the victim exposed,

269
00:21:40,640 --> 00:21:42,180
and often, of course, naked.

270
00:21:42,740 --> 00:21:45,480
And at that point, any number of things can be done.

271
00:21:45,481 --> 00:21:59,481
If the rack were to continue, there would be potentially ripping of tendons and ligaments and nerve damage that might be chronic and go on forever.

272
00:22:00,540 --> 00:22:06,380
People might feel the inability to use their hands or their lower extremities.

273
00:22:06,500 --> 00:22:10,620
They might feel numbness, constant pain in their fingertips or toes.

274
00:22:10,621 --> 00:22:16,120
Ultimately, the rack was designed to slowly tear the victim's limbs out of their joints.

275
00:22:16,121 --> 00:22:20,620
The legs out of the hip joints, the arms out of the shoulder joints.

276
00:22:20,621 --> 00:22:24,620
The design of torture instruments changed as technology became more advanced.

277
00:22:24,621 --> 00:22:30,620
During the 1800s, a chair, completely forged of hardened metal, made its appearance.

278
00:22:30,621 --> 00:22:35,620
Simply called the interrogation chair, it soon became common throughout Europe.

279
00:22:35,621 --> 00:22:38,960
The primary function would be to sit you down.

280
00:22:38,961 --> 00:22:42,620
You're sitting on these spikes, which are obviously not comfortable.

281
00:22:42,621 --> 00:22:45,620
This bar would lock down your chest.

282
00:22:45,621 --> 00:22:48,620
Your wrists would be held down as well.

283
00:22:48,621 --> 00:22:52,620
And then your legs would be clamped.

284
00:22:52,621 --> 00:22:57,620
Once sitting in the chair, chances are it's not going to be too long before you begin to talk.

285
00:22:57,621 --> 00:23:02,620
I would guess that certain people would begin to talk before they sat in the chair.

286
00:23:02,621 --> 00:23:04,620
I mean, it's very ominous. You wouldn't want to be here.

287
00:23:04,621 --> 00:23:09,240
here. The second major torture mechanism and design of the chair actually is that

288
00:23:09,241 --> 00:23:15,500
it can be heated. All the metal spikes that you see conduct heat. Fire will be

289
00:23:15,501 --> 00:23:21,660
placed underneath the chair bringing this intense hot feeling.

290
00:23:21,661 --> 00:23:25,240
Thumbscrews and finger screws were another highly engineered torture device.

291
00:23:25,241 --> 00:23:29,860
Machine tooled to mechanical precision, they worked like a pair of nutcrackers.

292
00:23:29,861 --> 00:23:42,640
The way it would be used would be to place the fingers or knuckles inside the two bars, slowly increasing pressure until the proper amount of pain was necessary to get

293
00:23:42,641 --> 00:23:46,620
the information that you might want from the victim.

294
00:23:46,621 --> 00:23:49,400
The thumb is the most important finger you have.

295
00:23:49,820 --> 00:23:55,440
So to actually crush the thumb makes you almost useless as a manual laborer.

296
00:23:55,620 --> 00:23:58,000
So it's got tremendous value in that way.

297
00:23:58,300 --> 00:24:01,520
It's also a very sensitive part of your body.

298
00:24:01,840 --> 00:24:04,560
The nerves are highly dense in the fingers,

299
00:24:04,800 --> 00:24:10,280
so that crushing nerves in the fingers causes significant pain.

300
00:24:11,860 --> 00:24:16,340
A few torture devices seem to go beyond ordinary tools of pain and suffering.

301
00:24:16,620 --> 00:24:20,740
Perhaps the most dreadful instrument of such debauchery was the pear.

302
00:24:21,560 --> 00:24:26,200
What's amazing about this piece in particular is the amount of work that went into it.

303
00:24:27,060 --> 00:24:31,820
It's made of bronze, it's cast, and it took a bit of ingenuity and technology,

304
00:24:31,960 --> 00:24:33,100
especially for the time period.

305
00:24:33,840 --> 00:24:36,800
Like many torture instruments, they're multifunctional.

306
00:24:37,840 --> 00:24:43,660
In an oral capacity, it would be placed into the mouth and slowly opened.

307
00:24:43,661 --> 00:24:47,600
for those who had uttered heretical comments.

308
00:24:48,360 --> 00:24:52,840
Now, for those who had committed homosexuality,

309
00:24:53,260 --> 00:24:54,960
it would be placed in the rectum

310
00:24:54,961 --> 00:24:57,680
and, again, slowly opened, increasing the pain.

311
00:24:58,360 --> 00:25:01,540
In the case of women, it would be placed in the vagina

312
00:25:01,541 --> 00:25:03,820
and, again, slowly opened

313
00:25:03,821 --> 00:25:09,680
and to the point where these points would puncture the uterus.

314
00:25:09,681 --> 00:25:18,440
Across the Atlantic, in the New World, Native American nations had mastered the use of torture

315
00:25:18,441 --> 00:25:22,642
devices that were far less elaborate than the Iron Maiden or even the RAC.

316
00:25:22,643 --> 00:25:23,380
But the basic

317
00:25:23,381 --> 00:25:28,040
reasons for torturing prisoners remained the same, to punish and to terrorize.

318
00:25:28,980 --> 00:25:34,640
In the East Coast, we do find lots of evidence of using shells to flay people alive, usually

319
00:25:34,641 --> 00:25:36,040
while they're tied to a stake.

320
00:25:36,520 --> 00:25:39,980
And oftentimes that was followed by burning or boiling

321
00:25:39,981 --> 00:25:42,200
the stripped flesh in front of the victim.

322
00:25:43,320 --> 00:25:46,800
Among the Algonquin, it was the men who would capture the victims

323
00:25:46,801 --> 00:25:47,620
and tie them up.

324
00:25:48,340 --> 00:25:51,300
But it was often left to the women to perform the actual torture.

325
00:25:54,460 --> 00:25:57,740
Some of the burning was akin to what was happening in Europe

326
00:25:57,741 --> 00:25:59,240
in terms of burning at the stake.

327
00:25:59,760 --> 00:26:04,160
Having a low heat fire slowly smolder under the victim's feet

328
00:26:04,161 --> 00:26:10,420
Other times the torturers would insert pieces of wood into various parts of the body and set them on fire.

329
00:26:10,880 --> 00:26:17,180
There's accounts of people placing them under the fingernails, in the mouth, in the ears, in the nose,

330
00:26:17,660 --> 00:26:23,260
anywhere where you could have a non-fatal burn to prolong the agony of the victim.

331
00:26:23,920 --> 00:26:27,600
Very often the length of the torture itself was determined by the victim's behavior.

332
00:26:28,240 --> 00:26:30,660
There was honor for those who stoically accepted the pain.

333
00:26:31,620 --> 00:26:35,320
It's the people who cried out, who expressed some sort of fear.

334
00:26:35,700 --> 00:26:39,560
They could expect a much longer torture session before ultimately being killed.

335
00:26:39,920 --> 00:26:42,380
But usually, death was the ultimate result.

336
00:26:44,120 --> 00:26:48,260
By the mid-19th century, in Europe, Great Britain, and the United States,

337
00:26:48,900 --> 00:26:51,680
there was a groundswell of popular sentiment against torture.

338
00:26:53,540 --> 00:26:57,000
In 1864, the Geneva Convention was drafted.

339
00:26:57,001 --> 00:27:02,340
It was an international agreement that, among other things, prohibited the use of torture.

340
00:27:04,460 --> 00:27:10,260
But rather than bringing the practice to an end, it would slowly drive it underground.

341
00:27:17,200 --> 00:27:22,100
By World War II, the practice of torture had been illegal for almost 80 years.

342
00:27:22,101 --> 00:27:31,700
the creators of torture devices should have been out of business instead they turned to everyday

343
00:27:31,701 --> 00:27:37,540
appliances items that could be found in the household or on the job the amazing thing is

344
00:27:37,541 --> 00:27:43,540
is the common devices the things that you use in your everyday life things that you find at home

345
00:27:43,541 --> 00:27:49,540
that become suddenly violent, sick devices of torture.

346
00:27:49,541 --> 00:27:54,540
A fork can be used to hold somebody's finger in place,

347
00:27:54,541 --> 00:27:58,540
to hold somebody's pinky in place, to stab somebody.

348
00:27:58,541 --> 00:28:01,540
The same with the, you know, the pliers.

349
00:28:01,541 --> 00:28:03,540
I mean, again, they're so simple,

350
00:28:03,541 --> 00:28:06,540
and yet they can create such agony.

351
00:28:06,541 --> 00:28:10,540
All of these things were used as torture devices by the Nazis,

352
00:28:10,541 --> 00:28:12,540
by the Gestapo.

353
00:28:13,540 --> 00:28:19,780
A famous French-born British spy named Odette was arrested by the Gestapo in Marseille in 1943.

354
00:28:21,160 --> 00:28:25,320
When she refused to answer her interrogators' questions about the French underground

355
00:28:25,321 --> 00:28:30,860
and the British-supported spy network operating in Vichy, France, Odette's torture began.

356
00:28:31,620 --> 00:28:36,900
They took her to an interrogation room, and they used a red-hot poker,

357
00:28:37,240 --> 00:28:41,300
and they touched it to her third or fourth lumbar on her spine and just held it there,

358
00:28:41,301 --> 00:28:46,380
burning her skin for up to minutes.

359
00:28:47,040 --> 00:28:51,240
And they pulled her fingernails out very slowly,

360
00:28:51,400 --> 00:28:54,280
fingers and toenails, one at a time, with pliers.

361
00:28:56,100 --> 00:28:59,360
In spite of the wretched pain and heavy discharge of blood,

362
00:29:00,080 --> 00:29:01,880
Odette refused to give up her comrades,

363
00:29:02,480 --> 00:29:04,220
who she knew faced certain death.

364
00:29:06,280 --> 00:29:08,740
Even though she was herself sentenced to death,

365
00:29:08,741 --> 00:29:15,740
Odette somehow turned up in a concentration camp for women and survived the war.

366
00:29:17,740 --> 00:29:24,740
Most Gestapo torture victims knew they were being tortured because they had verbal secrets their torturers wanted.

367
00:29:24,741 --> 00:29:35,740
But a small group of victims at Auschwitz concentration camp had no idea that they were being tortured because they had medical secrets locked away in their bodies.

368
00:29:35,741 --> 00:29:40,120
The notorious Dr. Josef Mengele tortured his victims in the name of science.

369
00:29:41,340 --> 00:29:45,460
The devices he employed were the common tools of a physician,

370
00:29:46,120 --> 00:29:48,440
scalpels, forceps, and surgical scissors.

371
00:29:49,580 --> 00:29:56,640
Really, he's remembered for his grotesque and vicious torture on the inmates,

372
00:29:57,260 --> 00:30:00,940
particularly twins, identical as opposed to fraternal.

373
00:30:00,941 --> 00:30:04,480
He would then perform various experiments,

374
00:30:04,481 --> 00:30:12,840
Some involved injecting different chemicals into the body of one twin and not the other and see how they react.

375
00:30:13,740 --> 00:30:19,280
He also wanted to take tissue samples from the major organs.

376
00:30:20,080 --> 00:30:24,020
And this was often done, you know, in the most brutal way.

377
00:30:26,640 --> 00:30:33,380
One of Mengele's most horrifying tortures was placing some twins into a vat of near-boiling water for a period of time

378
00:30:33,381 --> 00:30:35,740
and then strapping them onto a table

379
00:30:35,741 --> 00:30:39,760
so that the softened tissue samples could be more easily sliced away

380
00:30:39,761 --> 00:30:42,000
without the benefit of anesthesia.

381
00:30:42,800 --> 00:30:46,620
There were 125 sets of twins in Auschwitz in 1944.

382
00:30:47,620 --> 00:30:50,720
By the end of the year, only a dozen or so survived.

383
00:30:51,480 --> 00:30:53,420
This is how monstrous a man,

384
00:30:54,000 --> 00:30:57,440
the experiments and all of these things that he performed,

385
00:30:57,900 --> 00:30:59,520
this is his legacy, really.

386
00:30:59,521 --> 00:31:01,260
And, of course, he's never caught.

387
00:31:01,840 --> 00:31:02,780
I mean, he got away.

388
00:31:03,380 --> 00:31:07,740
Mengele vanished in 1945 when Allied forces liberated the camps.

389
00:31:08,480 --> 00:31:12,180
Despite international efforts to track him down, he was never apprehended.

390
00:31:12,920 --> 00:31:19,800
A 68-year-old man who died in Brazil in 1979 was later identified through DNA as Mengele.

391
00:31:26,120 --> 00:31:31,340
In the Pacific during World War II, wherever the Imperial Japanese forces were victorious,

392
00:31:31,341 --> 00:31:34,440
the people of that nation were often tortured.

393
00:31:34,441 --> 00:31:39,340
It made no difference if the victims were civilians or military POWs.

394
00:31:39,341 --> 00:31:46,340
The most common instruments of punishment were the canes and whips they used to flog their prisoners.

395
00:31:46,341 --> 00:31:50,340
Some POWs were hung upside down by their feet,

396
00:31:50,341 --> 00:31:55,340
while urine nor iodine was poured directly into their nostrils or down their throats.

397
00:31:55,341 --> 00:32:00,940
their throats.

398
00:32:00,941 --> 00:32:06,900
In January 1942, when the Japanese invaded the American-held Philippine Islands, American

399
00:32:06,901 --> 00:32:11,200
civilian expatriates were arrested and imprisoned.

400
00:32:11,201 --> 00:32:16,460
The first civilian the Japanese took into custody was a middle-aged, stubborn Kentuckian

401
00:32:16,461 --> 00:32:19,400
who had been slightly crippled from polio.

402
00:32:19,401 --> 00:32:24,400
His name was Roy Bennett, a respected journalist who had refused to stop criticizing the Japanese

403
00:32:24,401 --> 00:32:29,200
government's violent empire building.

404
00:32:29,201 --> 00:32:33,920
His wife and two daughters were also arrested and placed in the Santo Tomas internment camp

405
00:32:33,921 --> 00:32:36,720
in Manila.

406
00:32:36,721 --> 00:32:43,080
He had been the editor of the Manila Daily Bulletin, which was the most influential English-language

407
00:32:43,081 --> 00:32:46,080
newspaper in the Far East.

408
00:32:46,081 --> 00:32:52,520
And he, as an influential person, saying negative things against the Japanese, and he was very

409
00:32:52,521 --> 00:32:56,122
harsh, very loud, prior to their coming in.

410
00:32:56,123 --> 00:32:59,640
Bennett was incarcerated in Fort Santiago,

411
00:32:59,641 --> 00:33:03,148
an ancient dreary prison reserved for civilian political prisoners.

412
00:33:03,149 --> 00:33:04,300
It had been a fortress

413
00:33:04,301 --> 00:33:09,260
built by the Spanish when they were in charge of the Philippines, when they owned the Philippine

414
00:33:09,261 --> 00:33:10,107
Islands.

415
00:33:10,108 --> 00:33:18,260
And it was exactly what you would think of as a Spanish dungeon and fortress.

416
00:33:18,261 --> 00:33:23,060
Japanese captors wanted to use him as a propaganda tool to torture him into

417
00:33:23,061 --> 00:33:26,440
admitting he had been gravely misguided when he spoke out against the Japanese

418
00:33:26,441 --> 00:33:32,360
government. One of the tortures that was used on many of the political prisoners

419
00:33:32,361 --> 00:33:39,440
was inserting a bamboo sliver deep under the fingernail and then lighting it. But

420
00:33:39,441 --> 00:33:45,640
probably the most common physical torture was simply beating them. The beatings

421
00:33:45,641 --> 00:33:50,500
Things were administered by just about anything at hand, including their hands.

422
00:33:50,501 --> 00:34:00,080
They would use clubs, bamboo canes, the flat side of a bayonet, just anything that they

423
00:34:00,081 --> 00:34:04,780
could use to inflict great pain.

424
00:34:04,781 --> 00:34:09,540
As Bennett and other civilian, political, and military prisoners suffered, the Japanese

425
00:34:09,541 --> 00:34:13,340
were also torturing Filipinos who remained loyal to the Americans.

426
00:34:13,341 --> 00:34:19,440
It was not uncommon for a Japanese soldier to take a baby from its mother's arms, from

427
00:34:19,441 --> 00:34:25,640
its Filipino mother's arms, throw it in the air and catch it on the bayonet.

428
00:34:25,641 --> 00:34:34,640
It was not uncommon for the soldiers to rape the women, to execute or torture a Filipino

429
00:34:34,641 --> 00:34:41,040
man in front of his family, or to torture the Filipino wife and children in front of

430
00:34:41,041 --> 00:34:42,040
the man.

431
00:34:43,340 --> 00:34:49,980
On February 3, 1945, Roy Bennett and his family were among the prisoners liberated by the American forces.

432
00:34:52,620 --> 00:34:55,020
Two weeks later, they returned to the United States.

433
00:34:56,060 --> 00:35:00,940
The torture really never ends. It is always with the person who's been tortured.

434
00:35:02,380 --> 00:35:04,048
They relive it all the time.

435
00:35:04,049 --> 00:35:07,740
They have to decide why it is that they lived and others died.

436
00:35:07,741 --> 00:35:16,520
the screams are as real to them 20 years after the fact as they were when it was happening to them.

437
00:35:17,980 --> 00:35:24,560
According to his family, Roy Bennett carried the psychological scars of his torture until his death in 1967.

438
00:35:27,740 --> 00:35:32,860
35 years later, and 3,000 miles southwest by way of the South China Sea,

439
00:35:33,540 --> 00:35:35,440
a gruesome discovery was made.

440
00:35:35,441 --> 00:35:42,720
It was in Cambodia in 1978 that the invading Vietnamese army found Pol Pot's secret prison.

441
00:35:43,720 --> 00:35:45,440
He called it S-21.

442
00:35:46,620 --> 00:35:52,340
It was used as a secure location to torture Cambodian prisoners he believed were counter-revolutionaries.

443
00:35:52,740 --> 00:36:00,340
This was a secret place because the whole business of what the government was doing was kept a secret for most of the people in the country most of the time.

444
00:36:00,341 --> 00:36:06,080
particularly as if it involved torturing and killing people who were accused of being enemies of the state.

445
00:36:06,900 --> 00:36:09,600
A prison archive was meticulously maintained.

446
00:36:10,580 --> 00:36:16,800
Within its pages are entries that reveal appalling acts of torture carried out against its inmate population.

447
00:36:17,800 --> 00:36:23,740
What you have here is an interrogator's appendix to a confession.

448
00:36:23,741 --> 00:36:25,520
In other words, it's a rare kind of document.

449
00:36:25,521 --> 00:36:30,880
We get very few of these documents where an interrogator tells you what he did in sequence.

450
00:36:32,320 --> 00:36:38,020
On the morning of the 18th of August, 1977, I decided, this is the interrogator speaking,

451
00:36:38,120 --> 00:36:39,240
I decided to employ torture.

452
00:36:39,960 --> 00:36:43,480
I watched his morale fall while I administered torture, but he had no reaction.

453
00:36:44,020 --> 00:36:49,480
In the afternoon of the 21st, another day later, I pressured him again using the electric cord.

454
00:36:50,580 --> 00:36:53,800
He was given two or three spoonfuls of **** to eat.

455
00:36:54,420 --> 00:36:55,480
That would be human excrement.

456
00:36:55,860 --> 00:36:59,220
And after that, he was able to answer questions, etc.

457
00:36:59,221 --> 00:37:02,060
That night, I beat him again with the electric cord.

458
00:37:02,980 --> 00:37:05,280
It's an incredible quotation, as you see.

459
00:37:07,260 --> 00:37:09,500
Besides being forced to eat feces,

460
00:37:10,020 --> 00:37:11,820
prisoners also were forced to drink urine,

461
00:37:12,960 --> 00:37:14,860
were subjected to electric shocks

462
00:37:14,861 --> 00:37:18,080
and suffocation with a plastic bag.

463
00:37:20,440 --> 00:37:23,660
One of the many clients that I have worked with

464
00:37:23,661 --> 00:37:24,899
from Cambodia.

465
00:37:24,900 --> 00:37:32,420
He had a plastic bag put over his head during the course of an interrogation session.

466
00:37:32,800 --> 00:37:36,519
It was put over his head. They would ask him a question. He would not answer.

467
00:37:36,520 --> 00:37:37,340
They would put it

468
00:37:37,341 --> 00:37:39,825
over his head, and he would nearly suffocate.

469
00:37:39,826 --> 00:37:42,420
They would take it off briefly, interrogate him

470
00:37:42,421 --> 00:37:46,094
some more, put the bag over his head.

471
00:37:46,095 --> 00:37:51,060
The consequence of being in a bag is asphyxiation.

472
00:37:51,061 --> 00:37:56,060
The person who is asphyxiated loses consciousness.

473
00:37:56,061 --> 00:38:00,060
They may lose consciousness and come back too, and the torture continues.

474
00:38:00,061 --> 00:38:05,060
There may be a cycle of asphyxiation, losing consciousness, and coming back for more torture.

475
00:38:05,061 --> 00:38:10,060
They also have the possibility of being asphyxiated so badly that they die.

476
00:38:10,061 --> 00:38:16,060
The long-term consequences of asphyxiation would most likely include brain damage.

477
00:38:16,061 --> 00:38:23,820
In 1984, four years after the discovery of the horror of Pol Pot's secret prison, the

478
00:38:23,821 --> 00:38:28,900
United Nations General Assembly passed the UN Convention Against Torture.

479
00:38:28,901 --> 00:38:35,120
Even so, today the worldwide use of torture and torture devices continues to spin dangerously

480
00:38:35,121 --> 00:38:38,400
out of control.

481
00:38:38,401 --> 00:38:42,920
Experts tell us that torture and the use of torture devices is currently a worldwide

482
00:38:42,921 --> 00:38:45,300
epidemic.

483
00:38:45,301 --> 00:38:50,360
According to Amnesty International, 75% of the world's governments have employed torture

484
00:38:50,361 --> 00:38:53,700
since the beginning of the new millennium.

485
00:38:53,701 --> 00:38:59,140
Electronic shocking devices, beatings, and psychological abuse top the list.

486
00:38:59,141 --> 00:39:04,060
And there is an ever-increasing array of ordinary objects that are not technology-driven, but

487
00:39:04,061 --> 00:39:08,820
that are used as instruments of torture simply because they are available.

488
00:39:08,821 --> 00:39:12,140
Among the most disturbing may be sexual torture.

489
00:39:12,141 --> 00:39:14,940
vaginal penetration, sodomization.

490
00:39:15,400 --> 00:39:19,100
Men and women are subjected to rape as a form of torture.

491
00:39:21,280 --> 00:39:23,740
A famous example of that is the Coke bottle.

492
00:39:24,240 --> 00:39:26,460
Coca-Cola bottles were used in Latin America

493
00:39:26,461 --> 00:39:29,480
to be inserted in victims' anuses.

494
00:39:29,920 --> 00:39:30,620
Why?

495
00:39:30,621 --> 00:39:32,900
Because when the person was let go from the prison,

496
00:39:33,080 --> 00:39:34,740
the Coca-Cola bottle is ubiquitous.

497
00:39:34,960 --> 00:39:37,040
They could not go anywhere without seeing it

498
00:39:37,041 --> 00:39:39,200
and therefore being reminded of their torture.

499
00:39:39,201 --> 00:39:46,200
torture. The most accessible contemporary torture devices are fists, feet, whips and

500
00:39:46,201 --> 00:39:46,901
canes.

501
00:39:46,902 --> 00:39:51,200
It may be for this reason that beatings are among the most common form of torture in

502
00:39:51,201 --> 00:39:57,200
the world today, especially where there is religious persecution, in countries like Egypt,

503
00:39:57,201 --> 00:39:59,293
Pakistan, the Sudan and China.

504
00:39:59,294 --> 00:40:03,200
One famous form of torture that occurred for a long time

505
00:40:03,201 --> 00:40:09,200
and still occurs throughout the world is Falanga, which is the beating on the soles of the feet,

506
00:40:09,201 --> 00:40:10,800
with a blunt object like a cane.

507
00:40:10,801 --> 00:40:15,300
What happens short term is tremendous pain and bleeding

508
00:40:15,301 --> 00:40:17,200
in the bottoms of the feet.

509
00:40:17,201 --> 00:40:21,300
But after that heals, for months and even years afterwards,

510
00:40:21,301 --> 00:40:26,560
the torture survivor has pain just walking on their feet.

511
00:40:26,561 --> 00:40:28,140
Beatings are sometimes accompanied

512
00:40:28,141 --> 00:40:30,440
with degrading acts of obscenity.

513
00:40:30,441 --> 00:40:34,760
A somewhat typical incident involved three Ethiopian women.

514
00:40:34,761 --> 00:40:38,560
First of all, they were stripped of their clothing.

515
00:40:38,561 --> 00:40:42,100
They were hung upside down by a pole, just an ordinary pole.

516
00:40:43,040 --> 00:40:45,940
And then they were whipped with cords.

517
00:40:46,540 --> 00:40:49,680
When they screamed, they would have socks put in their mouths.

518
00:40:50,280 --> 00:40:54,420
Sometimes there would be bloody socks, sometimes vomit-filled socks.

519
00:40:54,520 --> 00:40:57,120
And this is a very typical form of torture.

520
00:40:58,340 --> 00:41:03,840
In my experience, there are a wide range of implements

521
00:41:03,841 --> 00:41:08,540
and other objects that have been used to torture individuals.

522
00:41:09,040 --> 00:41:16,100
To name a few, iron bars, sometimes that are heated up and applied to the body,

523
00:41:16,360 --> 00:41:21,720
cigarette butts, batons are quite common, rifle butts.

524
00:41:22,220 --> 00:41:27,040
I've had clients who have been, their bodies have been pressed against hot burning radiators.

525
00:41:28,960 --> 00:41:31,860
Often the key to the successful use of a torture device

526
00:41:31,861 --> 00:41:34,680
is the torturer's skill in keeping his victim alive.

527
00:41:36,660 --> 00:41:37,860
Persons who have been tortured,

528
00:41:38,440 --> 00:41:40,680
certainly those who are being tortured for political purposes,

529
00:41:41,000 --> 00:41:42,620
are sent out into the community

530
00:41:42,621 --> 00:41:45,160
as symbols of what will happen to others

531
00:41:45,161 --> 00:41:47,040
if they continue to be in opposition.

532
00:41:47,600 --> 00:41:48,940
You need to be alive for that.

533
00:41:48,941 --> 00:41:52,780
And you need for the community to see you as a broken person.

534
00:41:53,600 --> 00:41:57,040
The use of torture devices remains a shadowy business.

535
00:41:58,240 --> 00:42:01,320
While civilization's enemies may be arrested and tortured,

536
00:42:01,860 --> 00:42:05,360
legions of innocents are left to suffer the same fate,

537
00:42:05,361 --> 00:42:07,860
sometimes for years on end.

538
00:42:07,861 --> 00:42:10,860
With the threat of violence on the increase,

539
00:42:10,861 --> 00:42:14,860
it is likely the use of torture devices will increase as well.

540
00:42:14,861 --> 00:42:19,860
Ultimately, what people can really do to help resolve

541
00:42:19,861 --> 00:42:25,860
the issue of torture in our world is to gain a new understanding of it,

542
00:42:25,861 --> 00:42:30,860
to accept that it exists, and to become educated.

543
00:42:30,861 --> 00:42:34,460
Because it's real and the more educated we are about it,

544
00:42:34,461 --> 00:42:38,860
the more likely we are to do what we can to resolve it.

545
00:42:38,861 --> 00:42:42,860
This is a dark side of our humanity that we need to deal with and better understand.

546
00:42:53,860 --> 00:42:55,860
There are always going to be surprises.

547
00:42:57,360 --> 00:43:00,360
You don't have to speak the language to be able to interact with people.

548
00:43:00,860 --> 00:43:04,680
Knowing that can make you look at history in a new and exciting way.

